claim:
§5.1 permits the verifier 'A single trailing newline', but §5.2 requires the host to parse 'exactly one JSON object with no trailing bytes' and to reject any 'leading/trailing prefix or suffix around the object'. A byte-literal host rejects `{"verdict":"allow"}
` (a newline is a trailing byte); a host built on JSON.parse or serde_json::from_str accepts unlimited leading/trailing JSON whitespace (RFC 8259 defines JSON-text = ws value ws), so `

	{"verdict":"allow"}

` is accepted by the lazy host and rejected by the strict one. Neither `
` (is CRLF 'a single newline'?), leading whitespace, nor a doubled LF is addressed, and no vector pins any of them: host-deny-allow-trailing-garbage and host-deny-leading-garbage use non-whitespace bytes, so a host that passes all 29 vectors can be either strict or lenient. Fix: define the stdout grammar normatively — RECOMMENDED: verdict-text = ws* object ws* where ws is exactly the RFC 8259 whitespace set (SP, HT, LF, CR), any other byte outside the object is a prefix/suffix — and align §5.1 wording ('trailing JSON whitespace is OPTIONAL').
